1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,239
MARTHA: Hi Alex.

2
00:00:02,239 --> 00:00:07,580
It's Martha Clines here. James White gave me your number

3
00:00:07,580 --> 00:00:10,300
I hope you don't mind me calling you.

4
00:00:10,300 --> 00:00:13,500
ALEX: Of course not. How are you, Martha?

5
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:18,700
MARTHA: Good thanks. I'm ringing because I need a bit of advice.

6
00:00:18,700 --> 00:00:20,900
ALEX: Oh yeah. What about?

7
00:00:20,900 --> 00:00:25,020
MARTHA: The training you did at JPNW a few years ago

8
00:00:25,020 --> 00:00:27,850
I'm applying for the same thing.

9
00:00:27,850 --> 00:00:31,600
ALEX: Oh right. Yes, I did mine in 2014

10
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:34,750
Best thing I ever did. I'm still working there.

11
00:00:34,750 --> 00:00:38,200
MARTHA: Really? What are you doing?

12
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:41,520
ALEX: Well, now I work in the customer services department

13
00:00:41,520 --> 00:00:43,759
but I did my initial training in Finance.

14
00:00:43,759 --> 00:00:48,700
I stayed there for the first two years and then moved to where I am now.

15
00:00:48,700 --> 00:00:53,900
MARTHA: That's the same department I'm applying for. Did you enjoy it?

16
00:00:53,900 --> 00:00:56,290
ALEX: I was pretty nervous to begin with.

17
00:00:56,290 --> 00:00:59,120
I didn't do well in my exams at school

18
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:01,668
and I was really worried because I failed Maths.

19
00:01:01,668 --> 00:01:05,850
But it didn't actually matter because I did lots of courses on the job.

20
00:01:05,850 --> 00:01:09,480
MARTHA: Did you get a diploma at the end of your trainee period

21
00:01:09,480 --> 00:01:13,000
I'm hoping to do the one in business skills.

22
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:16,920
ALEX: Yes. That sounds good. I took the one on IT skills

23
00:01:16,920 --> 00:01:19,350
but I wish I'd done that one instead.

24
00:01:19,350 --> 00:01:24,780
MARTHA: OK, that's good to know. What about the other trainees

25
00:01:24,780 --> 00:01:27,200
How did you get on with them?

26
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:31,060
ALEX: There were about 20 of us who started at the same time

27
00:01:31,060 --> 00:01:34,244
and we were all around the same age -

28
00:01:34,244 --> 00:01:39,275
I was 18 and there was only one person younger than me, who was 17.

29
00:01:39,275 --> 00:01:43,300
The rest were between 18 and 20. I made some good friends.

30
00:01:43,300 --> 00:01:47,718
MARTHA: I've heard lots of good things about the training at JPNW.

31
00:01:47,718 --> 00:01:51,400
It seems like there are a lot of opportunities there.

32
00:01:51,400 --> 00:01:58,400
ALEX: Yeah, definitely. Because of its size you can work in loads of different areas within the organisation.

33
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:05,000
MARTHA: What about pay? I know you get a lower minimum wage than regular employees.

34
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:06,719
ALEX: That's right - which isn't great.

35
00:02:06,719 --> 00:02:10,157
But you get the same number of days' holiday as everyone else.

36
00:02:10,157 --> 00:02:12,500
And the pay goes up massively

37
00:02:12,500 --> 00:02:15,600
if they offer you a job at the end of the training period.

38
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:18,640
MARTHA: Yeah, but I'm not doing it for the money

39
00:02:18,640 --> 00:02:22,250
it's the experience I think will be really useful.

40
00:02:22,250 --> 00:02:26,800
Everyone says by the end of the year you gain so much confidence.

41
00:02:26,800 --> 00:02:30,040
ALEX: You're right. That's the most useful part about it

42
00:02:30,040 --> 00:02:32,353
There's a lot of variety too.

43
00:02:32,353 --> 00:02:34,840
You're given lots of different things to do

44
00:02:34,840 --> 00:02:38,600
I enjoyed it all - I didn't even mind the studying.

45
00:02:38,600 --> 00:02:41,650
MARTHA: Do you have to spend any time in college?

46
00:02:41,650 --> 00:02:43,880
ALEX: Yes, one day each month

47
00:02:43,880 --> 00:02:48,200
So you get lots of support from both your tutor and your manager.

48
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:53,800
MARTHA: That's good. And the company is easy to get to, isn't it?

49
00:02:53,800 --> 00:02:59,450
ALEX: Yes, it's very close to the train station so the location's a real advantage.

50
00:02:59,450 --> 00:03:02,600
ALEX: Have you got a date for your interview yet?

51
00:03:02,600 --> 00:03:06,500
MARTHA: Yes, it's on the 23rd of this month.

52
00:03:06,500 --> 00:03:12,150
ALEX: So long as you're well prepared there's nothing to worry about. Everyone's very friendly.

53
00:03:12,150 --> 00:03:17,400
MARTHA: I am not sure what I should wear. What do you think?

54
00:03:17,400 --> 00:03:20,600
ALEX: Nothing too casual - like jeans, for example.

55
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:25,800
If you've got a nice jacket, wear that with a skirt or trousers.

56
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:30,100
MARTHA: OK. Thanks. Any other tips?

57
00:03:30,100 --> 00:03:34,453
ALEX: Erm, well I know it's really obvious but arrive in plenty of time.

58
00:03:34,453 --> 00:03:37,120
They hate people who are late

59
00:03:37,120 --> 00:03:40,050
So make sure you know exactly where you have to get to.

60
00:03:40,050 --> 00:03:46,896
And one other useful piece of advice my manager told me before I had the interview for this job -

61
00:03:46,896 --> 00:03:52,300
is to smile. Even if you feel terrified. It makes people respond better to you.

62
00:03:52,300 --> 00:03:56,700
MARTHA: I'll have to practise doing that in the mirror!

63
00:03:56,700 --> 00:04:01,950
ALEX: Yeah - well, good luck. Let me know if you need any more information.

64
00:04:01,950 --> 00:04:04,700
MARTHA: Thanks very much.
